2
00:00:03,810 --> 00:00:06,030
Holloway and in this
session we're going

3
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:08,450
to be discussing
opportunistic mycoses.

4
00:00:12,190 --> 00:00:15,270
And here are the objectives
for the session today.

5
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:17,530
You should be able to
describe the different

6
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:21,210
fungal pathogens, including
the diseases they cause,

7
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:24,050
any risk factors
associated with them, and

8
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:26,890
then explain the diagnosis
and treatment of each.

9
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:32,470
Opportunistic mycoses
are fungi that don't

10
00:00:32,470 --> 00:00:35,950
produce disease in the vast
majority of immunocompetent

11
00:00:35,950 --> 00:00:38,610
people and almost
exclusively create

12
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:41,070
disease in those with
impaired immune defenses.

13
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:44,250
Now, unlike the primary
fungal pathogens,

14
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:46,830
these organisms are
distributed worldwide,

15
00:00:46,970 --> 00:00:49,530
found naturally either
in the environment

16
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:51,870
or existing as
commensal organisms.

17
00:00:52,510 --> 00:00:55,170
They tend to have low
inherent virulence

18
00:00:55,170 --> 00:00:57,470
and are generally
harmless to most people.

19
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:02,470
Now, when medical
interventions or medications or

20
00:01:02,470 --> 00:01:05,810
certain diseases weaken
the immune system, then

21
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:08,750
these have the potential
to cause disease, which

22
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:11,130
could actually be pretty
severe in certain cases.

23
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:15,170
Now, of course, besides
immunosuppression through

24
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:18,450
disease or medication,
other risk factors can lead

25
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:22,490
to infections too, like
severe skin damage through

26
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:26,750
burns or other trauma,
indwelling medical devices,

27
00:01:26,950 --> 00:01:30,130
or conditions like
diabetes, among others.

28
00:01:30,890 --> 00:01:33,230
And on the right side
there, you can see

29
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:35,130
the list of fungal
diseases that we're

30
00:01:35,130 --> 00:01:36,810
going to be covering
in the session today.

31
00:01:37,410 --> 00:01:41,130
Now, it's also worth noting
that the list of organisms

32
00:01:41,130 --> 00:01:44,990
and the incidence of serious
fungal disease will only

33
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:48,050
continue to increase as
medical advancements allow

34
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:51,230
patients with weakened immune
systems to live longer,

35
00:01:51,230 --> 00:01:54,750
which creates a larger and
more vulnerable population.

36
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:00,810
Okay, we'll start here
with the genus Candida that

37
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:03,050
contains species considered
to be part of the normal

38
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:06,990
microbiota, primarily
being found on the skin and

39
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:10,910
in the gastrointestinal
and genitourinary tracts.

40
00:02:11,410 --> 00:02:13,670
Of the 20 or so
species of candida

41
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:15,610
that can cause
infections in humans,

42
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:17,610
candida albicans is definitely

43
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:18,990
the most common species.

44
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:21,970
It's a polymorphic
fungus that switches

45
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:23,890
between oval budding yeast,

46
00:02:23,890 --> 00:02:26,770
elongated pseudohyphae,
and true hyphae.

47
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:30,770
Pseudohyphae form when
budding yeast cells elongate

48
00:02:30,770 --> 00:02:33,870
but remain attached in
chains, like you can see

49
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:36,570
in the middle of the image
here, which shows the

50
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:38,970
difference between all
three morphological forms.

51
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:41,710
Not all Candida species produce

52
00:02:41,710 --> 00:02:44,510
pseudohyphae, so the
morphological forms will

53
00:02:44,510 --> 00:02:47,090
vary, as will other
characteristics.

54
00:02:47,390 --> 00:02:51,190
So, for instance, Glabrata
usually displays more drug

55
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:55,490
resistance than albicans,
and likewise Candida auris

56
00:02:55,490 --> 00:02:58,230
is another species that's
even more drug resistant

57
00:02:58,230 --> 00:03:01,530
and can cause very serious
bloodstream infections.

58
00:03:02,130 --> 00:03:06,010
Now, when host defenses
are impaired or when

59
00:03:06,010 --> 00:03:08,310
there is a microbial
imbalance created where the

60
00:03:08,310 --> 00:03:12,430
organisms usually exist,
then a disease can result.

61
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:15,870
So having intact
immune defenses here is

62
00:03:15,870 --> 00:03:21,350
important, including a
functional cell-mediated

63
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:25,010
response along with a
robust neutrophil response,

64
00:03:25,010 --> 00:03:28,150
the lack of which can
actually act as a predisposing

65
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:30,870
factor to disseminated
candida infections.

66
00:03:34,370 --> 00:03:37,950
So here are some common
clinical manifestations of

67
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:41,710
candidiasis, beginning with
the oropharyngeal form,

68
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:45,170
otherwise known as oral thrush,
which is characterized by

69
00:03:45,170 --> 00:03:47,770
white patches inside the
mouth and throat, like you

70
00:03:47,770 --> 00:03:50,750
can see on the tongue of
the baby here in the image.

71
00:03:51,190 --> 00:03:54,090
An extension of oral
thrush down the throat can

72
00:03:54,090 --> 00:03:57,410
then lead to esophageal
candidiasis that often

73
00:03:57,410 --> 00:04:00,350
presents with difficult
and painful swallowing.

74
00:04:01,270 --> 00:04:03,490
***** ****** candidiasis
is definitely

75
00:04:03,490 --> 00:04:05,410
more common than the
oral form, though,

76
00:04:05,430 --> 00:04:09,370
which is a standard ******
yeast infection. This can

77
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:12,350
occur when other resident
microbes are disrupted.

78
00:04:12,350 --> 00:04:15,710
So, for instance, if
antibiotics are being used and

79
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:19,490
suppress normal bacterial
species like lactobacillus

80
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:22,710
that produce acid and keep
the environmental pH low.

81
00:04:23,050 --> 00:04:25,130
An increased pH
then creates a more

82
00:04:25,130 --> 00:04:27,150
favorable condition
for fungal growth.

83
00:04:27,510 --> 00:04:31,550
Skin infections or
cutaneous candidiasis will

84
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:35,130
generally appear as red and
itchy rashes on the skin.

85
00:04:35,290 --> 00:04:38,990
These are most common in
warm and moist areas of

86
00:04:38,990 --> 00:04:43,710
the body, like the armpits,
the groin area, or under

87
00:04:43,710 --> 00:04:45,870
the ******* like you can
see in the image here.

88
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:49,720
It's also a common cause
of diaper rashes, which

89
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:52,230
is just another moist
and warm environment.

90
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:57,330
And those will produce
a red rash, sometimes

91
00:04:57,330 --> 00:05:01,290
wet and glossy looking,
with satellite dots or

92
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:03,630
lesions that are scattered
just outside the main

93
00:05:03,630 --> 00:05:05,730
rash, like you can see
in the picture here.

94
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:08,850
It's also possible for
fingers and nails to become

95
00:05:08,850 --> 00:05:11,690
infected when they're
repeatedly immersed in water.

96
00:05:15,430 --> 00:05:19,270
Chronic mucocutaneous
candidiasis is caused by an

97
00:05:19,270 --> 00:05:22,130
immune deficiency and
usually characterized by

98
00:05:22,130 --> 00:05:25,290
persistent infections
of the skin, nails, and

99
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:28,150
mucous membranes, just like
we talked about before.

100
00:05:28,390 --> 00:05:31,790
Now, this can happen because
of a genetic immune system

101
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:35,090
disorder, which is marked
by mutations disrupting

102
00:05:35,090 --> 00:05:39,150
the IL-17 signaling pathway,
which makes patients highly

103
00:05:39,150 --> 00:05:41,750
susceptible to these types
of localized infections.

104
00:05:42,470 --> 00:05:46,370
Disseminated candidiasis, on
the other hand, is a severe

105
00:05:46,370 --> 00:05:49,050
systemic infection where
the fungus spreads through

106
00:05:49,050 --> 00:05:52,710
the bloodstream to other
tissues and can form abscesses

107
00:05:52,710 --> 00:05:57,170
on organs like the spleen,
liver, or the kidneys.

108
00:05:57,510 --> 00:06:00,770
This form is associated
with risk factors like

109
00:06:00,770 --> 00:06:05,350
being immune compromised,
the use of medical devices

110
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:09,130
that allow access to blood
or activities like IV

111
00:06:09,130 --> 00:06:12,450
drug use which also provides
access to the blood.

112
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:15,350
Dissemination has the
potential to cause

113
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:19,310
complications as well
like meningitis or

114
00:06:19,310 --> 00:06:22,390
endocarditis or even
infection in the eye.

115
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:28,950
Diagnosis can be done by
visualizing it microscopically

116
00:06:28,950 --> 00:06:32,490
from samples and being able
to identify pseudohyphae

117
00:06:32,490 --> 00:06:35,030
like you could see here
in the image on the left.

118
00:06:35,490 --> 00:06:38,710
Candida albicans will also
produce germ tubes when

119
00:06:38,710 --> 00:06:41,550
grown at 37 degrees like
you could see in the image

120
00:06:41,550 --> 00:06:44,650
on the right, so culturing
it is an option too.

121
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:47,850
Now both of those would
be diagnostic and it

122
00:06:47,850 --> 00:06:50,110
could be further differentiated
from other species

123
00:06:50,110 --> 00:06:52,920
by the use of carbohydrate
fermentation reactions

124
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:55,330
and of course there's
always the use of molecular

125
00:06:55,330 --> 00:06:57,750
methods like PCR to
help with diagnostics.

126
00:06:58,570 --> 00:07:00,370
Treatment for
Candidiasis depends

127
00:07:00,370 --> 00:07:02,470
on where the
infection is located.

128
00:07:03,290 --> 00:07:06,150
The common options
are topical creams

129
00:07:06,150 --> 00:07:08,710
and suppositories for
****** infections,

130
00:07:08,870 --> 00:07:11,570
usually azoles
like myconazole or

131
00:07:11,570 --> 00:07:14,950
clotrimazole, and oral
rinses for thrush.

132
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:19,030
Nystatin or other azoles
would be used for the

133
00:07:19,030 --> 00:07:22,550
skin, like diaper
rashes, and any sort of

134
00:07:22,550 --> 00:07:25,210
persistent or disseminated
infection might then

135
00:07:25,210 --> 00:07:28,610
require a combination
therapy with Amphotericin

136
00:07:31,670 --> 00:07:34,490
The term aspergillosis
refers to any

137
00:07:34,490 --> 00:07:37,690
illness caused by a
species of aspergillus,

138
00:07:37,690 --> 00:07:41,170
usually fumigatus,
flavus, or niger.

139
00:07:42,090 --> 00:07:44,210
That could be lung invasion,

140
00:07:44,270 --> 00:07:46,990
cutaneous infection,
or dissemination.

141
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:49,690
Aspergillus species
are ubiquitous in

142
00:07:49,690 --> 00:07:51,690
the environment, and
the inhalation of

143
00:07:51,690 --> 00:07:53,910
canidia is pretty
frequent for people.

144
00:07:54,050 --> 00:07:54,730
However,

145
00:07:54,910 --> 00:07:56,950
disease is really
only seen in a

146
00:07:56,950 --> 00:07:58,710
situation of immunosuppression.

147
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:01,810
Aspergillus is not
considered part of the

148
00:08:01,810 --> 00:08:05,050
normal flora and exists
only in mold form.

149
00:08:05,650 --> 00:08:08,470
Morphologically, they
have septate hyphae,

150
00:08:08,470 --> 00:08:10,610
which means that you can
see the perpendicular

151
00:08:10,610 --> 00:08:12,790
barriers along the
length of a filament.

152
00:08:13,050 --> 00:08:16,370
And they also form V-shaped
branches, like you can

153
00:08:16,370 --> 00:08:19,130
see here in the stained
microscopic image up top.

154
00:08:19,510 --> 00:08:22,610
You can see a long black
arrow that points to

155
00:08:22,610 --> 00:08:25,690
the septate hyphae, and
a short black arrow that

156
00:08:25,690 --> 00:08:28,190
points to the typical
Y-shaped branch point.

157
00:08:29,390 --> 00:08:32,610
Aspergillus also has
identifiable canidiophores,

158
00:08:32,610 --> 00:08:34,430
which are the
specialized filaments

159
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:36,970
that terminate in many
chains of canidia,

160
00:08:36,970 --> 00:08:39,340
like you can see here
in the bottom images.

161
00:08:43,230 --> 00:08:46,670
Any condition that
compromises the effectiveness

162
00:08:46,670 --> 00:08:50,110
of the immune response
will be a risk factor here.

163
00:08:50,730 --> 00:08:53,870
So transplants,
cancer treatments,

164
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:58,090
the use of corticosteroids,
and also having

165
00:08:58,090 --> 00:09:00,830
low levels of neutrophils,
which is actually a

166
00:09:00,830 --> 00:09:03,890
main defense. These can
all lead to disease.

167
00:09:04,030 --> 00:09:07,090
Or also having a pre
-existing condition like

168
00:09:07,090 --> 00:09:09,730
tuberculosis, where
the fungus can grow in

169
00:09:09,730 --> 00:09:12,270
the cavities created
by the prior infection.

170
00:09:13,850 --> 00:09:16,130
Pathogenesis
starts with inhaled

171
00:09:16,130 --> 00:09:19,430
canidia, usually
from fumigatus, and

172
00:09:19,430 --> 00:09:22,030
those canidia then
evade phagocytosis.

173
00:09:22,230 --> 00:09:25,790
The canidia are going to
germinate into hyphae, and

174
00:09:25,790 --> 00:09:29,070
then they can invade blood
vessels, causing tissue

175
00:09:29,070 --> 00:09:32,950
necrosis, and potentially
can disseminate systemically.

176
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:35,030
Now, once in the
bloodstream, the

177
00:09:35,030 --> 00:09:36,670
infection can spread
from the lungs to

178
00:09:36,670 --> 00:09:38,550
virtually any
location in the body.

179
00:09:38,670 --> 00:09:42,570
And disseminated aspergillosis
has a very high mortality

180
00:09:42,570 --> 00:09:46,230
rate and requires pretty
urgent therapy so overall

181
00:09:46,230 --> 00:09:48,650
the clinical presentation
is going to vary based

182
00:09:48,650 --> 00:09:52,830
on the host immune response
and any any sort of underlying

183
00:09:52,830 --> 00:09:59,210
lung condition that there
might be acute invasive

184
00:09:59,210 --> 00:10:03,110
aspergulosis which is the
most severe and rapid form

185
00:10:03,110 --> 00:10:07,190
will have symptoms like a
fever a cough that might

186
00:10:07,190 --> 00:10:11,090
have blood in it and chest
pain and this could quickly

187
00:10:11,090 --> 00:10:13,910
become life-threatening. In
these cases, the infection

188
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:16,570
usually invades blood
vessels and then spreads.

189
00:10:16,910 --> 00:10:20,030
An infection can also be
chronic when it's confined

190
00:10:20,030 --> 00:10:22,870
to a single area,
which is typically the

191
00:10:22,870 --> 00:10:26,790
lungs, and where it might
then form into fungal

192
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:31,090
balls or aspergolomas that
develop in the cavities

193
00:10:31,090 --> 00:10:33,450
within the lungs, like
you could see here in the

194
00:10:33,450 --> 00:10:36,650
image, which is indicated
by the black arrow.

195
00:10:36,810 --> 00:10:40,350
These can be seen on a chest
x-ray and they can also

196
00:10:40,350 --> 00:10:43,990
shift position in the cavity
when a patient is moved.

197
00:10:44,150 --> 00:10:47,190
A localized infection
can also occur

198
00:10:47,190 --> 00:10:49,750
in the sinuses as
opposed to the lungs.

199
00:10:52,810 --> 00:10:56,650
In the disseminated form
of the disease, multiple

200
00:10:56,650 --> 00:10:58,650
different areas of the
body can be affected

201
00:10:58,650 --> 00:11:04,150
like the skin, the
eyes, liver, kidneys, or

202
00:11:04,150 --> 00:11:06,450
the brain like you could
see here in the image.

203
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:10,670
The fungus can then produce
abscesses or necrotic

204
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:13,870
lesions at these sites,
and this type of infection

205
00:11:13,870 --> 00:11:16,930
is usually associated with
a very poor prognosis.

206
00:11:18,110 --> 00:11:23,570
Okay, a different
manifestation, ABPA, is a

207
00:11:23,570 --> 00:11:27,210
hypersensitivity reaction in
the bronchi. Now, patients

208
00:11:27,210 --> 00:11:29,150
that get this typically
will also have a

209
00:11:29,150 --> 00:11:33,270
predisposing condition like
asthma or cystic fibrosis.

210
00:11:33,290 --> 00:11:36,190
In these cases, the
fungus lives in the

211
00:11:36,190 --> 00:11:39,780
airways without invading
the lung tissue, but

212
00:11:39,990 --> 00:11:43,750
it will instead cause
swelling, thick mucus,

213
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:46,250
and lung damage if
it's left untreated.

214
00:11:46,990 --> 00:11:49,710
Immunologically, the
patients will often display

215
00:11:49,710 --> 00:11:54,090
increased levels of IgE and
eosinophils, and and they

216
00:11:54,090 --> 00:11:56,910
might also cough up mucus
that contains the fungus.

217
00:12:00,730 --> 00:12:03,410
Diagnosis could be
done using samples that

218
00:12:03,410 --> 00:12:06,390
show septate Y-shaped
branching hyphae,

219
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:07,870
like you can see in the image

220
00:12:07,870 --> 00:12:09,590
here that we discussed earlier.

221
00:12:10,270 --> 00:12:12,910
And culturing can
also be used, but it's

222
00:12:12,910 --> 00:12:14,690
not necessarily
definitive because

223
00:12:14,690 --> 00:12:17,010
people can have
transient colonization.

224
00:12:17,770 --> 00:12:19,870
Serology could be
utilized here as

225
00:12:19,870 --> 00:12:22,610
well to detect
galactamannan, which is

226
00:12:22,610 --> 00:12:24,670
a sugar component
of the cell wall.

227
00:12:25,130 --> 00:12:29,030
And patients with ABPA, as
we mentioned before, will

228
00:12:29,030 --> 00:12:33,190
have high levels of IgE
specific for aspergillus

229
00:12:33,190 --> 00:12:37,230
antigens and will also
have increased eosinophils.

230
00:12:38,450 --> 00:12:42,170
Voriconazole is the drug
of choice for invasive

231
00:12:42,170 --> 00:12:46,430
aspergillosis and
various other antifungals

232
00:12:46,430 --> 00:12:48,700
will be used as second
-line therapeutics.

233
00:12:49,250 --> 00:12:52,630
Surgery is also an option
if a fungal ball is

234
00:12:52,630 --> 00:12:55,790
growing in the sinuses
or in a pulmonary cavity.

235
00:12:59,090 --> 00:13:01,910
Cryptococosis is an
infection due to either

236
00:13:01,910 --> 00:13:05,430
Cryptococcus neophormans
or Cryptococcus gadii,

237
00:13:05,430 --> 00:13:08,390
but neophormans is
seen more frequently.

238
00:13:08,530 --> 00:13:10,950
There are four different
serotypes between the

239
00:13:10,950 --> 00:13:13,650
two species that are
designated A through D.

240
00:13:14,070 --> 00:13:16,390
Now, the cells classically
are going to produce

241
00:13:16,390 --> 00:13:19,610
a very thick polysaccharide
capsule, which happens

242
00:13:19,610 --> 00:13:21,590
to be shed by the
organism during the course

243
00:13:21,590 --> 00:13:24,750
of infection and can be
detected serologically.

244
00:13:25,070 --> 00:13:27,770
And you can see the
clear halo encasing

245
00:13:27,770 --> 00:13:30,490
each cell represented
here in both images.

246
00:13:31,190 --> 00:13:33,870
Now, of course, the
capsule, which is similar to

247
00:13:33,870 --> 00:13:36,210
many capsules, is going
to have antiphagocytic

248
00:13:36,210 --> 00:13:39,410
properties and is a very
important virulence factor.

249
00:13:40,230 --> 00:13:44,330
the yeast cells reproduce
by budding a process that's

250
00:13:44,330 --> 00:13:47,210
going to produce a narrow
based bud like you can see

251
00:13:47,210 --> 00:13:50,250
here in the black and
white image which is useful

252
00:13:50,250 --> 00:13:53,030
for differentiating
cryptococcus from other fungal

253
00:13:53,030 --> 00:13:57,150
infections in addition to
the large capsule that it has

254
00:13:57,190 --> 00:14:01,710
now the risk factors for
cryptococcus infection are

255
00:14:01,710 --> 00:14:05,250
really going to mirror
other opportunistic fungi so

256
00:14:05,250 --> 00:14:08,830
some sort of immunosuppression,
organ transplant, etc.

257
00:14:10,090 --> 00:14:13,550
From an environmental
standpoint, cryptococcus is

258
00:14:13,550 --> 00:14:17,690
associated with birds,
specifically pigeons. It

259
00:14:17,690 --> 00:14:20,630
can be found globally in
soil samples associated

260
00:14:20,630 --> 00:14:23,870
with birds and where they
roost. Now, it doesn't

261
00:14:23,870 --> 00:14:26,710
necessarily infect the
birds. They just harbor

262
00:14:26,710 --> 00:14:29,890
the yeast in their
gastrointestinal tract, and

263
00:14:29,890 --> 00:14:32,710
then it's going to be shed
in their feces, which can

264
00:14:32,710 --> 00:14:35,690
then become aerosolized
and inhaled by people.

265
00:14:39,510 --> 00:14:42,830
Cryptococcus infection
is usually asymptomatic

266
00:14:42,830 --> 00:14:46,010
for healthy individuals,
but could also produce

267
00:14:46,010 --> 00:14:49,710
a mild pneumonia-like
illness with symptoms like

268
00:14:49,710 --> 00:14:52,730
a cough, chest pain,
and shortness of breath.

269
00:14:52,930 --> 00:14:55,990
Now, generally, the
infection will be cleared and

270
00:14:55,990 --> 00:15:00,330
symptoms will resolve,
but infections can be much

271
00:15:00,330 --> 00:15:03,710
more acute and severe in
immunocompromised patients.

272
00:15:04,530 --> 00:15:07,630
Dissemination from the
lung can occur, showing

273
00:15:07,630 --> 00:15:10,490
some variety of skin
lesion, but much more

274
00:15:10,490 --> 00:15:12,950
importantly, dissemination
from the lungs will often

275
00:15:12,950 --> 00:15:15,590
locate to the central
nervous system of

276
00:15:15,590 --> 00:15:20,510
immunocompromised patients,
causing meningitis, which is

277
00:15:20,510 --> 00:15:23,530
a much more chronic and
slowly progressive form

278
00:15:23,530 --> 00:15:28,030
with symptoms like severe
headache and fever,

279
00:15:28,190 --> 00:15:29,830
nausea and vomiting,

280
00:15:29,990 --> 00:15:32,510
and ultimately
neurological complications

281
00:15:32,510 --> 00:15:35,590
that can be fatal if
it's left untreated.

282
00:15:36,030 --> 00:15:41,210
Now, both species can also
cause cryptococomas during

283
00:15:41,210 --> 00:15:44,990
the infection, where the
body forms a granuloma

284
00:15:44,990 --> 00:15:48,370
attempting to wall off the
infection in a local area

285
00:15:50,690 --> 00:15:54,130
all right diagnosis can be
done by negative staining

286
00:15:54,130 --> 00:15:57,790
using a dye like india ink
to visualize the cells with

287
00:15:57,790 --> 00:16:00,710
their very thick capsule
microscopically like you

288
00:16:00,710 --> 00:16:04,450
can see here in the image
and you could also culture

289
00:16:04,450 --> 00:16:08,950
it as well serological
testing could be used too for

290
00:16:08,950 --> 00:16:13,190
both the antibody and the
antigen a specific latex

291
00:16:13,190 --> 00:16:16,430
particle agglutination test
can detect an increased

292
00:16:16,430 --> 00:16:19,510
amount of capsular
antigen and fluid like the

293
00:16:19,510 --> 00:16:24,470
cerebrospinal fluid for severe
infections like meningitis

294
00:16:24,470 --> 00:16:28,470
and other disseminated
types amphotericin b and

295
00:16:28,470 --> 00:16:31,420
flucytosine are going to be
used in combination fluconazole

296
00:16:32,610 --> 00:16:36,350
is used in aids patients
for long-term suppression

297
00:16:36,350 --> 00:16:40,090
of cryptococcal meningitis
but it can also be used

298
00:16:40,090 --> 00:16:42,910
to treat mild and moderate
lung infections as well

299
00:16:47,170 --> 00:16:50,110
Pneumocystis was
originally classified as a

300
00:16:50,110 --> 00:16:53,400
protozoal organism,
but was subsequently

301
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:57,090
reclassified as a fungus
based on its RNA profile

302
00:16:57,090 --> 00:17:00,250
and also the composition
of its cell wall.

303
00:17:00,570 --> 00:17:02,010
In addition,

304
00:17:02,150 --> 00:17:05,630
the species name was
formerly Carinii,

305
00:17:05,630 --> 00:17:08,050
but has since been
changed to the species

306
00:17:08,050 --> 00:17:11,650
name Uravetsi, which
was done to distinguish

307
00:17:11,650 --> 00:17:14,510
it from the species
that infect rats.

308
00:17:15,490 --> 00:17:18,810
pneumocystis is transmitted
through the air by

309
00:17:18,810 --> 00:17:22,850
person-to-person spread
and individuals with normal

310
00:17:22,850 --> 00:17:26,710
immune systems can have
asymptomatic lung colonization

311
00:17:26,710 --> 00:17:30,170
and also serve as a
reservoir for the spread

312
00:17:30,170 --> 00:17:33,170
of pneumocystis to
immunocompromised individuals.

313
00:17:33,630 --> 00:17:36,630
Then within the lung
the organism is going to

314
00:17:36,630 --> 00:17:39,250
make cysts like you can
see in the image here.

315
00:17:39,750 --> 00:17:43,670
The patients that are at
highest risk are going to

316
00:17:43,670 --> 00:17:48,550
be those with HIV having
a reduced number of CD4 T

317
00:17:48,550 --> 00:17:52,590
cells, which is a predisposing
factor, and others

318
00:17:52,590 --> 00:17:57,050
with immune suppression
are also at elevated risk.

319
00:18:00,270 --> 00:18:04,190
Pneumocystis infection
causes pneumonia, and

320
00:18:04,190 --> 00:18:07,780
the acronym PCP originally
stood for pneumocystis

321
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:10,570
Carinii pneumonia which
is named after the

322
00:18:10,570 --> 00:18:13,270
organism thought to cause
the disease in humans

323
00:18:13,690 --> 00:18:16,070
but as I mentioned it
was later identified

324
00:18:16,070 --> 00:18:18,790
as a different species
and then was renamed

325
00:18:19,050 --> 00:18:21,530
but rather than changing
the well-known acronym

326
00:18:21,530 --> 00:18:24,670
the pcp designation
was just adjusted to

327
00:18:24,670 --> 00:18:28,070
stand for pneumocystis
pneumonia with some

328
00:18:28,070 --> 00:18:31,570
also using the acronym
pjp to refer to it now

329
00:18:32,190 --> 00:18:34,790
This type of pneumonia
usually presents

330
00:18:34,790 --> 00:18:37,770
with shortness of
breath, fever, and a non

331
00:18:37,770 --> 00:18:40,630
-productive cough, and
if left untreated,

332
00:18:40,630 --> 00:18:43,270
it can lead to fatal
respiratory failure.

333
00:18:44,030 --> 00:18:47,510
Chest imaging often
shows diffuse bilateral

334
00:18:47,510 --> 00:18:50,850
infiltrates, or what's
said to be ground glass

335
00:18:50,850 --> 00:18:53,370
opacities, like you can
see here in the image.

336
00:18:53,810 --> 00:18:57,340
And in extremely rare
cases, it can occasionally

337
00:18:57,340 --> 00:18:59,510
disseminate to other
organs as well.

338
00:19:03,570 --> 00:19:06,290
Diagnosis could be done
by demonstrating the

339
00:19:06,290 --> 00:19:09,990
organism in a bronchial
fluid sample, which

340
00:19:09,990 --> 00:19:11,950
could be accomplished
with different staining

341
00:19:11,950 --> 00:19:14,490
methods, like you can
see here in the image.

342
00:19:14,730 --> 00:19:19,550
Panel A is a lung biopsy
with H&E staining, B is a

343
00:19:19,550 --> 00:19:23,300
silver stain, C is a
Gemsah stain, and D shows

344
00:19:23,300 --> 00:19:26,810
immunofluorescent staining, all
of which accomplish the goal.

345
00:19:27,270 --> 00:19:31,530
In general, this is not
considered to be culturable

346
00:19:31,530 --> 00:19:34,670
using standard microbiology
laboratory techniques.

347
00:19:35,270 --> 00:19:38,730
The drug of choice
is trimethoprim for

348
00:19:38,730 --> 00:19:42,210
pneumonia, with pentamidine
used prophylactically

349
00:19:42,210 --> 00:19:44,890
in patients with
low CD4 counts.

350
00:19:48,290 --> 00:19:51,070
Mucormycosis, which is
our last opportunistic

351
00:19:51,070 --> 00:19:53,990
example here, manifests in a

352
00:19:53,990 --> 00:19:56,230
variety of different
conditions in humans.

353
00:19:56,410 --> 00:19:59,550
most often in those with
pre-existing diseases.

354
00:19:59,900 --> 00:20:02,930
The two genera that
most commonly cause

355
00:20:02,930 --> 00:20:05,730
infections are
Rhizopus and Mucor.

356
00:20:06,230 --> 00:20:09,970
The hyphae that they
produce are distinct, like

357
00:20:09,970 --> 00:20:12,910
you can see here in the
microscopic image. They

358
00:20:12,910 --> 00:20:16,850
tend to be broad, they
have rare septations, and

359
00:20:16,850 --> 00:20:20,830
are irregularly branched
at somewhat right angles.

360
00:20:21,350 --> 00:20:23,950
Now, the organisms are fairly

361
00:20:23,950 --> 00:20:25,530
ubiquitous in the environment.

362
00:20:25,710 --> 00:20:28,630
They're found in the
soil, they grow rapidly,

363
00:20:28,630 --> 00:20:31,370
and they release
lots of spores that

364
00:20:31,370 --> 00:20:34,410
could become airborne
and are easily inhaled.

365
00:20:34,650 --> 00:20:38,550
Now, as such, all people
have ample exposure to

366
00:20:38,550 --> 00:20:40,950
them during just common
day-to-day activities.

367
00:20:41,330 --> 00:20:44,790
And the fact that this
disease is so rare shows

368
00:20:44,790 --> 00:20:47,590
how effective an intact
immune system can be.

369
00:20:48,730 --> 00:20:52,530
Almost all patients with
invasive mucormycosis have

370
00:20:52,530 --> 00:20:56,630
some underlying disease
that predisposes them. with

371
00:20:56,630 --> 00:21:00,350
a common situation being
immunosuppression from HIV,

372
00:21:00,470 --> 00:21:03,350
transplants,
therapeutics, as well as

373
00:21:03,350 --> 00:21:06,610
diabetes, specifically
with ketoacidosis.

374
00:21:06,830 --> 00:21:10,550
Now, the organisms have
an enzyme that allows them

375
00:21:10,550 --> 00:21:14,610
to thrive in high-glucose
acidic conditions, so the

376
00:21:14,610 --> 00:21:17,670
serum from individuals
with diabetic ketoacidosis

377
00:21:17,670 --> 00:21:21,690
stimulates growth. In
addition, these patients often

378
00:21:21,690 --> 00:21:24,610
have elevated concentrations
of free iron in their

379
00:21:24,610 --> 00:21:28,710
serum, which also supports
growth. And in general,

380
00:21:28,710 --> 00:21:32,590
iron overload itself may
predispose to mucormycosis

381
00:21:32,590 --> 00:21:36,170
because increased iron
uptake by the fungus can

382
00:21:36,170 --> 00:21:39,570
stimulate growth and can
also lead to tissue invasion.

383
00:21:42,810 --> 00:21:46,310
Disease here is associated
with tissue necrosis

384
00:21:46,310 --> 00:21:48,290
that results from
the invasion of blood

385
00:21:48,290 --> 00:21:51,790
vessels by the hyphae and
usually occurs quickly.

386
00:21:52,850 --> 00:21:55,390
Rhinoorbital cerebral
disease is the

387
00:21:55,390 --> 00:21:57,310
most common clinical
presentation.

388
00:21:57,490 --> 00:21:59,990
Now, after the
inhalation of spores, the

389
00:21:59,990 --> 00:22:04,030
infection usually presents
as acute sinusitis with

390
00:22:04,030 --> 00:22:07,510
nasal congestion,
headache, and sinus pain.

391
00:22:07,730 --> 00:22:11,650
The area can then swell as
it spreads throughout the

392
00:22:11,650 --> 00:22:16,110
sinuses and into the orbit of
the eye, and a black eschar

393
00:22:16,110 --> 00:22:19,430
can form due to tissue
necrosis after vascular

394
00:22:19,430 --> 00:22:23,630
invasion, both of which you
can see in the top two images.

395
00:22:24,130 --> 00:22:26,490
CNS involvement can also occur

396
00:22:26,490 --> 00:22:28,250
as it spreads from the sinuses.

397
00:22:29,250 --> 00:22:33,470
Pulmonary mucormycosis occurs
after inhalation of the

398
00:22:33,470 --> 00:22:37,790
spores into the bronchioles
and the alveoli and can be

399
00:22:37,790 --> 00:22:43,230
rapidly progressive as an
infection marked by a cough,

400
00:22:43,230 --> 00:22:47,390
chest pain, and fever, leading
then to tissue necrosis

401
00:22:47,390 --> 00:22:50,030
in the lungs like you can
see in the lower image.

402
00:22:51,270 --> 00:22:54,150
Infection of the skin and
soft tissues can occur as

403
00:22:54,150 --> 00:22:57,810
well if the spores access
a wound or some sort of

404
00:22:57,810 --> 00:23:02,610
abrasion, and that usually
begins as painful cellulitis,

405
00:23:02,610 --> 00:23:06,930
but can lead to rapid
progression of tissue necrosis.

406
00:23:08,750 --> 00:23:12,190
Gastrointestinal tract
infections can occur as a

407
00:23:12,190 --> 00:23:15,750
result of ingestion of the
spores, although it's rare,

408
00:23:15,750 --> 00:23:19,650
but that would be associated
with abdominal pain,

409
00:23:19,650 --> 00:23:23,070
nausea, vomiting, and
could lead to perforations.

410
00:23:23,590 --> 00:23:26,330
Also rare is
disseminated disease,

411
00:23:26,330 --> 00:23:28,950
most commonly
involving the brain.

412
00:23:29,330 --> 00:23:31,690
The prognosis in both of those

413
00:23:31,690 --> 00:23:34,390
instances is
generally pretty poor.

414
00:23:37,810 --> 00:23:40,930
Diagnosis usually relies
on the identification

415
00:23:40,930 --> 00:23:44,290
of the organism in
tissue biopsies, like

416
00:23:44,290 --> 00:23:47,290
you can see here in the
image, which is lung

417
00:23:47,290 --> 00:23:50,310
tissue stained with H
&E showing the hyphae.

418
00:23:51,060 --> 00:23:54,060
Culture could also be
used to confirm, but

419
00:23:54,060 --> 00:23:56,410
it's often difficult to
do because the hyphae

420
00:23:56,410 --> 00:24:00,010
are non-septate and
fragile, so they break

421
00:24:00,010 --> 00:24:03,230
easily during tissue
processing, and that then

422
00:24:03,230 --> 00:24:05,510
will prevent them from
growing in culture.

423
00:24:05,910 --> 00:24:09,490
But PCR can also be used
to confirm a diagnosis.

424
00:24:10,130 --> 00:24:14,730
Now, treatment of mucormycosis
can begin with the

425
00:24:14,730 --> 00:24:18,230
surgical removal of any
involved necrotic tissue,

426
00:24:18,230 --> 00:24:22,310
if possible, in combination
with starting antifungal

427
00:24:22,310 --> 00:24:25,630
therapy with a drug
like amphotericin B. So

428
00:24:39,860 --> 00:24:42,220
that concludes the
lecture. I just wanted

429
00:24:42,220 --> 00:24:44,340
to thank everyone
for listening, and I

430
00:24:44,340 --> 00:24:46,400
wanted to provide the
link for feedback.